('shipibo_applicative_record', '200 "Applicative Constructions In Shipibo-Konibo (Panoan)" by Pilar Valenzuela Skip to main content Chapman University Digital Commons Home About FAQ My Account < Previous Next > Home > Wilkinson > World Languages and Cultures > World Languages and Cultures Faculty Articles and Research > 2 World Languages and Cultures Faculty Articles and Research Applicative Constructions In Shipibo-Konibo (Panoan) Authors Pilar Valenzuela , Chapman University Follow Document Type Article Publication Date 2010 Abstract This article provides a detailed, typologically informed treatment of applicative constructions in Shipibo-Konibo, a Panoan language from Peruvian Amazonia. Shipibo-Konibo has three applicative suffixes: affective (i.e., benefactive or malefactive), dedicated malefactive, and associative. These applicative types are rather common cross-linguistically and hence the language cannot be said to be particularly rich either in terms of number or kinds of applicative constructions. Nevertheless, the Shipibo-Konibo system exhibits certain points of special interest such as the interplay between transitivity and the different applicative construction types, which include a restriction on the de', [('Download', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1001&context=language_articles'), ('download Adobe Reader', 'https://get.adobe.com/reader/'), ('Download', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1001&context=language_articles'), ('Rights and Terms of Use', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/termsofuse.pdf')])
